WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.622
The guarantees showed
what a caller can observe;

2
00:00:03.622 --> 00:00:06.809
this episode asks how
replicas compare histories.

3
00:00:07.159 --> 00:00:12.365
This is Clocks and version vectors:
causal order without a shared clock.

4
00:00:13.365 --> 00:00:15.606
Local wall clocks can be skewed.

5
00:00:15.606 --> 00:00:20.369
A timestamp alone cannot prove one
replica has seen another's write.

6
00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:25.513
A version vector records causal history,
not application data.

7
00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:28.915
It keeps one logical clock
for each replica.

8
00:00:29.265 --> 00:00:33.620
Each replica advances its own entry
when it records a noteworthy event.

9
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:37.423
It also carries forward clocks
from histories it has observed.

10
00:00:37.773 --> 00:00:44.055
For example, A's vector can read A two,
B one; B's can read A one, B three.

11
00:00:44.405 --> 00:00:45.671
Compare each entry.

12
00:00:45.671 --> 00:00:48.137
A is ahead on A, and B is ahead on B.

13
00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:52.802
Each has information the other lacks,
so the histories are concurrent.

14
00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:58.552
Merge by taking the maximum clock
in every replica entry: A two, B three.

15
00:00:58.552 --> 00:01:02.251
Deliver that vector again,
and it does not change.

16
00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:07.382
One vector dominates another when it is
at least as high in every entry.

17
00:01:07.382 --> 00:01:10.503
It has seen all the history
the other contains.

18
00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:15.218
The check is component-wise:
compare A with A and B with B,

19
00:01:15.218 --> 00:01:17.511
not the total of their numbers.

20
00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:22.763
A large value in one entry cannot make up
for a smaller one in another.

21
00:01:22.763 --> 00:01:26.832
The per-replica positions
preserve the causal frontier.

22
00:01:27.182 --> 00:01:32.093
The entries use hybrid logical clocks,
but the vector compares each replica's

23
00:01:32.093 --> 00:01:36.239
own history rather than relying
on one shared wall-clock reading.

24
00:01:36.589 --> 00:01:41.951
In Orleans.Lattice, the typed accessor can
tick a named replica and read the vector;

25
00:01:41.951 --> 00:01:46.292
the returned vector can test
that relation with `DominatesOrEquals`.

26
00:01:46.642 --> 00:01:49.605
The vector gives
the application a causal signal;

27
00:01:49.605 --> 00:01:53.415
the application still chooses
how to resolve concurrent values.

28
00:01:54.415 --> 00:01:57.133
Back on Evaluate, next: The evidence.

29
00:01:57.133 --> 00:02:01.393
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
